   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the state administrative procedure act and the economic\ndevelopment law, in relation to requiring the division for small-business to\npublish a small business compliance guide",
    "basePrintNo": "S7012",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7012-2023",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §202-e, St Ad Proc Act; add §138-a, Ec Dev L",
    "lawSection": "State Administrative Procedure Act",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S7012\n\nSPONSOR: MANNION\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the state administrative procedure act and the economic\ndevelopment law, in relation to requiring the division for small-busi-\nness to publish a small business compliance guide\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to require the Division for Small Business\nto compile and publish a small business compliance guide by January 31\nof each year that catalogs the statutory and regulatory changes of the\nprevious year in layman terms, provided that such guide shall be posted\nto the Division's website.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of this bill would require an annual small business compliance\nguide to be published in the State Register.\n\nSection 2 of this bill would require the Division for Small Business to\ncompile and publish a small business compliance guide by January 31 of\neach year that catalogs the statutory and regulatory changes of the\nprevious year in layman terms. Additionally, this section would require\nthe Commissioner of Economic Development to engage in a public awareness\ncampaign to inform small business owners about the guide and other\nresources available to assist with compliance.\n\nSection 3 of this bill provides that the effective date shall take\neffect immediately.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nUnder current law, the State is not statutorily required to create a\nsmall business compliance guide informing business owners of statutory\nand regulatory changes in one easy-to-understand guidance document.\nRather, the current law requires every agency to submit to the Secretary\nof State a list of all guidance documents on which the agency relies and\nhow the public may obtain such documents. Such guidance documents may\nalso be exempt from publication in the State Register if the documents\nare available on the agency's website.\n\nSmall businesses must comply with scores of local, State, and federal\nlaws, rules, and regulations, which are constantly changing. Such chang-\nes are often conveyed in formal, technical, and complex terms.  Keeping\nup with these changes is a burden for business owners.  Therefore, it is\nimperative to ease that burden by collecting many of the statutory and\nregulatory changes of the previous year in a small business compliance\nguide. This bill would make this burden easier for small business owners\nto consult one resource about changes made to laws and regulations\nduring the previous year.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2019-20: S.6800-A (Metzger)- Vetoed Memo.72.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\nTo be determined.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
